; Leg KS -- HW_TEST_16aq Part 2.  THE HOST RECEIVES.  The Victor sends
; FIXTURE.DAT by name.
;
; NOTE THE DIRECTION.  This is the only leg in the sheet where the host is
; the receiver, so START THIS BEFORE typing STEPKS on the Victor -- the
; Victor is the initiator here and will give up if nothing answers its S
; packets.  Same as SS16ah leg BS.
;
; THIS IS A REGRESSION CHECK, NOT A MEASUREMENT.  ttinl() is the PACKET
; READER, so on a send leg the only thing it reads is the ACK stream --
; tiny packets, no long buffered runs, and the bulk arm should be very
; nearly inert.  What this leg proves is that the arm does not break the
; direction it was not designed for.
;
; THE INTERESTING FAILURE IS A SURPRISE, NOT A CRASH.  Expect "bulk sel=1"
; with a SMALL n.  If n comes back large on a send leg, the reasoning behind
; this whole sheet is wrong somewhere -- it would mean the arm is finding
; long runs in a stream that should not have any -- and that is worth
; understanding BEFORE running Part 3, because Part 3's prediction rests on
; the same reading of what the arm does.
;
; FIXTURE.DAT, NOT TRANS.DAT.  There is no TRANS.DAT on this image.  The
; tree root holds TRANS.DAT.~1~ through ~3~ with the same md5
; (d94d2beda069ef0ef340977e7fd6995d), which is what makes the wrong name
; easy to write and is why the first draft of the sheet had it.
;
; TWO READING TRAPS, both from SS16ah leg BS and both still true:
;   - "wire=" in the Victor's .OUT divides rxbytes, so on a send leg it
;     reports the ACK STREAM over the whole elapsed time.  It is NOT the
;     send rate.  The host's statistics is the send rate.
;   - pktstat.py MISREADS send logs: its "longest packet" reads the one-byte
;     LEN field, which is 0 for long packets, and its S- count is the HOST
;     retransmitting.  Read s16aqKS.pkt by hand.
;
; The binary is CKBULK.EXE, 226,330 bytes, md5 a9fa6b5c...

set speed 38400
set receive timeout 20
set parity none
set prefixing cautious
set block 3
log packets s16aqKS.pkt
receive gotks.dat
statistics
